-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- Tenant-#2 readiness, part 4: make the credentials FK columns nullable to
-- match the ORM and actual application behaviour.
--
-- THE DEFECT
-- ----------
-- credentials.owner_id, access_level_id and request_id are NOT NULL in the
-- database (0000_rapid_onslaught.sql:105-108) but nullable in the ORM
-- (packages/db/src/schema/requests.ts:91-99, none carry .notNull()). The
-- application writes NULL to all three on purpose:
--
--   * owner_id     -- transitionRequest CANCEL unallocates a programmed fob:
--                     `UPDATE credentials SET owner_id = NULL` (requests.ts:374).
--   * access_level_id -- issueCredential inserts `resolvedAccessLevelId ?? null`
--                     when no access level resolves (requests.ts:1272).
--   * all three    -- bulk-add creates inventory fobs with only tenant_id,
--                     credential_type_id, serial_number, status, issued_at
--                     (fobs/bulk-add/route.ts:66-72) -- no owner, request, or
--                     access level.
--
-- Under the database's NOT NULL constraints every one of those paths raises
-- `null value in column ... violates not-null constraint`. The cross-tenant
-- isolation suite hit the owner_id case first (cancelling a request with a
-- linked active credential); the other two are the same latent bug on shipped
-- features. Production almost certainly already has these columns nullable
-- (dropped out of band, never migrated -- the same drift that left
-- users.firebase_uid and email_configurations unmigrated), which is why those
-- features work in prod but the migration-built CI/DR schema rejects them.
--
-- THE FIX
-- -------
-- Drop the three NOT NULL constraints so the database matches the ORM and the
-- code. DROP NOT NULL is idempotent-safe: a no-op where the column is already
-- nullable (production), corrective on a migration-built fresh database (CI,
-- DR restore). No consumer code changes are needed -- the ORM already types
-- all three as nullable, so every one of the ~40 files that read ownerId
-- already compiles against `string | null`.
--
-- The foreign keys and the credentials_owner_idx index are unaffected: a
-- nullable FK column is valid, and NULLs are simply not indexed / not checked.
-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

ALTER TABLE "credentials" ALTER COLUMN "owner_id" DROP NOT NULL;
--> statement-breakpoint
ALTER TABLE "credentials" ALTER COLUMN "access_level_id" DROP NOT NULL;
--> statement-breakpoint
ALTER TABLE "credentials" ALTER COLUMN "request_id" DROP NOT NULL;
